      "writtenOn": "2024-07-01T17:26:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If this code need to be thread safe, that implies the stack is handling multiple incoming request in parallel ? I don\u0027t think the code is architectured with this in mind. If we encounter failure at this level, I believe we should investigate and fix the threading issue more than having some thread safe class.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 12,
        "startChar": 70,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 4
      },
      "revId": "70eb745ab84abd397b766a5c5543a5234ec81946",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e4fb2bc1_c4e0beec",
        "filename": "system/common/sequential_id_generator.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 60,
      "author": {
        "id": 1881060
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-01T17:26:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure we need to have a full class to handle this, you could have a static method anywhere you need it that can do that too\n(and without mutex)\n\n```\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\nT getUniqueID() {\n   static std::atomic\u003cT\u003e unique_count(1);\n\n   T new_val \u003d 0;\n   do {\n      T cur_val \u003d unique_count;\n      if (cur_val \u003d\u003d std::numeric_limits\u003cT\u003e::max()) {\n        new_val \u003d 1\n      } else {\n        new_val \u003d cur_val + 1;\n      }\n   } while(!std::atomic_compare_exchange_weak(\u0026unique_count, \u0026cur_val, new_val));\n\n   return new_val;\n}\n```",
